---
/*
About: why Crossfire exists (to show what Odin can do for games and the tools around them), what
it's made of, the license.
*/
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { REPOS } from '../site';

// Lines of Odin in each part, counted on 2026-10-22 (comments and blank lines included)
const parts = [
	{ name: 'The engine', lines: '55,000', what: 'Scenes and nodes, rendering on SDL3, Box2D physics, audio, input, animation, lighting, particles, UI, tilemaps, the project format, the player and the web build.', repo: REPOS.engine },
	{ name: 'The editor', lines: '53,000', what: 'The scene editor, inspector, browser, the animation, timeline, state machine and dialogue panels, play and live view, exports, Git, plugins.', repo: REPOS.editor },
	{ name: 'The code editor', lines: '23,000', what: 'A code editor with an Odin language server client: completion, signatures, diagnostics, go to definition, find and replace, multiple cursors.', repo: `${REPOS.editor}/tree/main/code_editor` },
	{ name: 'Skald', lines: '53,000', what: 'The editor\'s UI framework: Elm-style (state, messages, views), drawn with Vulkan on SDL3 windows.', repo: REPOS.skald },
];
---

<SiteLayout title="About Crossfire" description="Why Crossfire exists: a 2D engine, its editor, the editor's code editor and UI framework, and your game's scripts, all written in Odin.">
	<section class="cf-section cf-page-head">
		<div class="cf-wrap">
			<p class="cf-kicker">About</p>
			<h1 class="cf-h2">One language. <span class="cf-fire-text">All the way down.</span></h1>
			<p class="cf-lead">
				Crossfire exists to show what Odin can do. Not a toy demo: a 2D engine and the complete
				tool you'd make games with, where every layer is Odin, and you can read all of it.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="cf-wrap cf-prose">
		<h2>Why Odin</h2>
		<p>
			<a href="https://odin-lang.org">Odin</a> is a systems language built for the kind of
			programs games are: data-oriented, fast, and close to the machine, but pleasant to write. It
			has the things game code wants without ceremony: custom allocators and arenas, slices and
			dynamic arrays, struct embedding with <code>using</code>, tagged unions, array programming
			for vectors (<code>position += velocity * dt</code>), reflection, and bindings for SDL,
			Vulkan, Box2D and friends in its vendor library.
		</p>
		<p>
			Most engines make you pick: a fast native core in one language, scripting in another, the
			editor in a third, with glue between them. Crossfire asks what happens if you don't. The
			answer is a stack with no seams: a script's struct is the same kind of struct the engine
			uses, the inspector reads its fields with Odin's reflection, and the debugger, profiler and
			compiler see one program.
		</p>

		<h2>What it's made of</h2>
		<p>About 184,000 lines of Odin across four parts, all open source:</p>
	</section>

	<section class="cf-wrap cf-about-parts">
		{parts.map((p) => (
			<a class="cf-card" href={p.repo}>
				<span class="cf-card-icon">{p.lines}</span>
				<h3>{p.name}</h3>
				<p>{p.what}</p>
			</a>
		))}
	</section>

	<section class="cf-wrap cf-prose">
		<h2>Your game is Odin too</h2>
		<p>
			A game's scripts are an Odin package. Each script is a struct that embeds{' '}
			<code>e.Script</code>, registers itself, and gets <code>ready</code>, <code>update</code> and{' '}
			<code>draw</code> calls; fields tagged <code>export</code> become inspector controls. The editor
			compiles them into a player made for your project, runs it as a separate process (a crash
			can't take the editor down), and with hot reload rebuilds it when you save, putting the game
			back where it was.
		</p>
		<p>
			Even the editor's plugins are Odin: a package in your project's <code>plugins/</code> folder,
			built as a shared library and loaded into the editor, adding commands, importers and
			inspector rows.
		</p>

		<h2>How it's built</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Tested.</strong> Hundreds of unit tests across the engine and editor, and UI test scripts that drive the real editor with injected input on every change.</li>
			<li><strong>Built in CI on every platform.</strong> Each build compiles and tests on Linux, Windows and macOS, then packages an AppImage, an installer and a disk image.</li>
			<li><strong>Documented.</strong> Every public procedure and type of the engine has a doc comment with an example that compiles; the <a href="/api/">API reference</a> is generated from them, and the <a href="/manual/">editor manual</a> covers every feature.</li>
			<li><strong>No hidden dependencies.</strong> SDL is linked in statically; a desktop game exports as one executable.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Where it's going</h2>
		<p>
			Crossfire is in active development: builds are published as development releases while the
			feature set settles. Version 1.0 will bring stable project files, a signed macOS app and more
			export targets. Follow along, try it, and <a href="/contribute/">tell us what breaks</a>.
		</p>

		<h2 id="license">License</h2>
		<p>
			Crossfire is free and open source under the <strong>MIT license</strong>: use it for anything,
			commercial games included, change it, ship it. The engine and editor bundle a few
			third-party pieces under their own permissive licenses (SDL, Box2D, FreeType, Font Awesome,
			and others), listed in each repository's README.
		</p>
		<pre class="cf-wrap-pre"><code>Copyright (c) 2026 Brandon Crowe

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and
associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal in the Software without restriction,
including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute,
sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: the above copyright notice and this
permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.

THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND. See LICENSE in each repository
for the full text.</code></pre>
	</section>
	<div style="height: 5rem"></div>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
	.cf-about-parts {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
		gap: 1px;
		background: var(--cf-line);
		border: 1px solid var(--cf-line);
		margin-block: 1.5rem 1rem;
	}
	.cf-about-parts .cf-card-icon { width: auto; padding-inline: 0.6rem; }
</style>
